course: dictionary
lesson: 4384
title: Hackles and body language
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Hackles are the hairs or feathers along the back of the neck of a dog, cat or bird. They stand up when the animal is angry or frightened. Hackles is a noun, and it is usually used in the plural.
explain: «Fur standing up along a dog's back is called raised hackles.» Raised hackles show the dog is very worked up. That can mean fear, anger or just big excitement, so look at the other clues before you decide how the dog feels.
explain: We also use hackles to talk about people. To raise someone's hackles, or make their hackles rise, means to make them angry. «Her hackles rose» and «put someone's hackles up» use the animal picture to show someone getting cross.
explain: Animals talk with their whole body, so read several clues together. A stiff, high, fast tail wag can mean a dog is tense, and raised hackles add to that picture. One clue alone can mislead you; several clues together tell the story.
fact: Hackles are hairs or feathers on the back of the neck of a dog, cat or bird.
fact: «Hackles» means the hairs or feathers that stand up when an animal is angry or frightened.
fact: To raise someone's hackles means to make them angry.
fact: Raised hackles can show fear, anger or big excitement, so check the other clues around the word.
fact: Dogs talk with their whole body: ears, tail, hackles, mouth and how they stand.
exercise: «Fur standing up along a dog's back is called raised hackles.» What are hackles? | Loose tails that wag when a dog is happy | A dog's favourite toy | *Hairs or feathers along the neck that stand up | The teeth a dog uses to bite
exercise: «Her hackles rose when her brother took the last biscuit.» What does this show? | She is very sleepy | *She is annoyed or angry | She is lost in the park | She is cold and wet
exercise: Raised hackles can show fear, anger or big excitement. What should you do to find out which one it is? | Stop reading the story | *Look at the other clues, like ears, tail and stance | Decide straight away that the animal is angry | Ask the animal's owner to guess for you
test: Ben says «put someone's hackles up» must mean to tidy their hair. What does it really mean? | To brush their hair | To give them a hat | To make them fall asleep | *To make them angry
test: «The bird's hackles stood up when the cat came near.» What does this suggest? | *The bird is frightened or alarmed | The bird is having a bath | The bird is very sleepy | The bird wants to sing
exam: What is the best way to work out the meaning of an unknown word like hackles in a story? | Skip it and never come back to it | Look up every word before you read on | Ask someone to read the whole story aloud for you | *Read the clues around it, guess the meaning, then check it fits the sentence
